. *********************************
. *********************************
. 
. * HYPOTHESIS TEST FOR H1
. 
.         *****************************************************************************************
> ************************************************************************
.         * We assume that the causal effect of having a daughter is identified in a sample of pare
> nts of a single child without further adjustment (see PAP)
.         * !! NOTE THIS IS THE AVERAGE CAUSAL EFFECT IN THE POPULATION OF PARENTS OF A SINGLE CHIL
> D !!
.         * We further assume that agreement to statements "Women should rather care about family t
> han career" [plh0301] and "Men should involve in housework" [plh0303] 
.         * are good measures of support for gender equality (hypothesis 1): 
.         * Less agreement to the former and more agreement to the latter is more likely to indicat
> e support for gender equality.
.         * Accordingly, we run a seemingly unrelated ordered probit regression analysis with famvs
> career [plh0301] and menhousework [plh0303] as dependent variables
.         * and the dummy variable daughter as independent variable in a sample of respondents with
>  a single child (parity2!=1)
.         * We apply weights cross-sectionally calibrated weights (phrf)
.         * To account for non-independence of observatons, we cluster standard errors at the house
> hold level
.         * The null we test against is that the effect of having daughters is <=0 on both outcomes
.         * I.e., we have to consider a one-sided test adjusted for multiple testing
.         * Since the used method provides a joint confidence band, this is equal to assessing whet
> her there is at least 1 significant effect
.         * We report all coefficients and statistics but only export the result with the smallest 
> p-value to comply to the provided template
.         * !! NOTE THAT AT AN A PRIORI CONFIDENCE LEVEL OF 0.05 WITHOUT PREDERMINED SIGN OF THE EF
> FECT, THE CONFIDENCE LEVEL BECOMES 0.025
.         *****************************************************************************************
> ************************************************************************
.         * The below code follows the conception outlined in the PAP
.         * There are two "technical" deviations from the preliminary code also provided in the PAP
> :
.         * suest takes iweights rather than pweights
.         * the sample restriction is already implemented in the data preparation and therefore omi
> tted for redundancy
.         *****************************************************************************************
> ************************************************************************
. 
. 
